{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[C]When [Am]I's in old Fort Worth in [F]eighteen and [C]eighty-three
[C]This old Mexican [Am]Cowboy come [F]stepin' up to [C]me
[C]Sayin' [Am]how are you, young [F]fella, how would you like to [C]go
[C]And [Am]spend another [F]season 'neath them hills of [C]Mexico?
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[C]Well, [Am]I had no employment and [F]back to him did [C]say
[C]It's accordin' to your [Am]wages, it's [F]accordin' to your [C]pay
[C]Said I'd [Am]pay to you good [F]wages and often, too, you [C]know
[C]If you'll [Am]spend another [F]season 'neath the hills of [C]Mexico
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[C]Now with [Am]all this Flatterin' talkin' he [F]signed up quite a [C]train
[C]Some [Am]ten or twelve in [F]number, all able bodied [C]men
[C]And our [Am]trip it was [F]pleasant as we hit the western road[C]
[C]Till we [Am]crossed the old Peace [F]River to those hills of [C]Mexico
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[C]It was [Am]there our pleasures [F]ended and our troubles all [C]begun
[C]Was a [Am]lightening storm that [F]hit us and made the cattle [C]run
[C]And we [Am]all got full of [F]stickers from the cactus that did [C]grow
[C]And the [Am]outlaws there did [F]rob us 'neath those hills of [C]Mexico
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
[C]Now the [Am]round-up's over [F]and that cowboy would not [C]pay,
[C]He said [Am]we'd been so [F]extravagant that we's in his debt that [C]day.
[C]But we [Am]showed him about [F]the punchers, that bankrupt would not [C]go,
[C]So we [Am]left that feller's [F]bones to bleach in the hills of [C]Mexico
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
[C]Well, they [Am]sent along that [F]old steamboat and back to home [C]did go
[C]How those [Am]bells started [F]ringing, them whistles they did [C]blow
[C]Going [Am]back to friends and [F]loves ones and I'll tell them not to [C]go
